WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.440
CI/CD compromise can turn a development pipeline into an attack path.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:10.020
Source control permissions, runner environments, deployment keys, artifact repositories,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:12.320
and build scripts all need protection.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.260
Insecure deserialization allows attackers to manipulate serialized input and influence

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:19.560
object state or server behavior.

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.900
This can lead to control bypass, data tampering, or code execution in vulnerable patterns.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:30.320
Unsigned code and unverified artifacts make it difficult to prove that deployed software

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:33.180
is authentic and unchanged.

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:37.320
Integrity validation should cover the path from source to build to release.

00:00:37.540 --> 00:00:42.740
Build pipeline attacks are especially relevant when automation deploys to systems that

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.600
handle CUI. Change control, separation of duties, approvals,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.940
and monitoring help keep the pipeline trustworthy.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.360
Use code signing and artifact integrity validation.

00:00:54.580 --> 00:00:57.700
Secure build pipelines with least privilege and approvals.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.920
Restrict and monitor changes to software and deployment processes.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:10.700
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include SI.L2-3.14.6 - Integrity verification;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:16.280
CM.L2-3.4.5 - Change control restrictions.
